00:12:57.800 --> 00:13:02.600 position:78%
and it constantly provides a new compilation that.

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:04.680
Even partial compilations.

00:13:04.840 --> 00:13:05.720
Even partial comp.

00:13:05.720 --> 00:13:08.653 position:86%
Typically when you type, by definition you are you know

00:13:08.706 --> 00:13:11.319 position:77%
it's like you've written half of the code, you or

00:13:11.373 --> 00:13:13.559 position:64%
less, and then typically doesn't compile.

00:13:13.559 --> 00:13:16.309 position:78%
Most of the time when you're in the compiler, what

00:13:16.364 --> 00:13:19.279 position:83%
you have written in your it does not compile exactly,

00:13:19.334 --> 00:13:20.159
so it requires.

00:13:20.440 --> 00:13:23.819 position:86%
It requires the background compilation to be able to do

00:13:23.881 --> 00:13:24.679
some partial.

00:13:24.679 --> 00:13:27.264 position:94%
Compilations for the utilis and something like that would be

00:13:27.307 --> 00:13:28.039
good at recovery.

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:30.720 position:83%
That's actually one of the harder parts because it's.

00:13:30.760 --> 00:13:34.145 position:80%
I mean, it's textbook to create a compiler that has

00:13:34.212 --> 00:13:37.598 position:80%
a perfect source, but but having a source where you

00:13:37.664 --> 00:13:41.249 position:84%
are typing something in the middle but you would still

00:13:41.316 --> 00:13:45.100 position:89%
like to have intellisense for what comes after means that

00:13:45.166 --> 00:13:46.760
you need to do recovery.

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:51.495 position:84%
And another important aspect of that is also that when

00:13:51.563 --> 00:13:55.122 position:81%
you only make more changes, you don't want to repass

00:13:55.191 --> 00:13:58.749 position:81%
everything, you want to reuse as much as possible to

00:13:58.818 --> 00:14:02.239 position:78%
be memory efficient in the background compilation.

00:14:02.240 --> 00:14:05.408 position:78%
Yeah, so it's you have good performance and a good

00:14:05.472 --> 00:14:07.119
experience as a developer.

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:10.699 position:83%
And that's actually one of the places where we really

00:14:10.754 --> 00:14:13.981 position:92%
benefited from what we got from Roslyn, because it's helped

00:14:14.036 --> 00:14:15.239
us be efficient there.

00:14:17.480 --> 00:14:21.593 position:89%
And the language services also an integral part of Visual

00:14:21.665 --> 00:14:25.706 position:88%
Studio Code and how they envision that you extend Visual

00:14:25.778 --> 00:14:28.159
Studio Code with other languages.

00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:31.478 position:88%
So that's the architecture they recommend if you want to

00:14:31.536 --> 00:14:32.999
implement a new language.

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:36.890 position:92%
Yes, and they actually invented what is called the Language

00:14:36.953 --> 00:14:39.079
Service Protocol exactly for this.

00:14:39.680 --> 00:14:45.809 position:95%
And it's a description of the communication between an editor

00:14:45.909 --> 00:14:48.120
and between languages.

00:14:48.440 --> 00:14:51.660 position:66%
And the idea is that as long as you adhere

00:14:51.736 --> 00:14:55.416 position:75%
to the LSP, you can replace languages or you can

00:14:55.493 --> 00:14:56.720
replace a sense.

00:14:57.320 --> 00:15:00.200 position:95%
That's actually a standard of an open source protocol, isn't.

00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:03.491 position:72%
It it is an open source protocol and it's very

00:15:03.563 --> 00:15:07.284 position:81%
efficient and it it's it's they come often come with

00:15:07.355 --> 00:15:10.718 position:73%
new additions to it that we can then uptake and

00:15:10.790 --> 00:15:14.439 position:80%
we can add support for that in our language server.

00:15:14.440 --> 00:15:17.972 position:95%
So we suddenly support new features invented in Visual Studio

00:15:18.030 --> 00:15:18.319
Code.

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:22.583 position:81%
So when when we, when we started modernizing the old

00:15:22.650 --> 00:15:27.180 position:97%
development environment and and starting you know, kind of revamping

00:15:27.247 --> 00:15:31.110 position:91%
the language, we we could have chosen an existing language

00:15:31.177 --> 00:15:35.374 position:98%
like we could have chosen, I don't know, TypeScript, JavaScript

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:36.439
or even Python.

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:38.416 position:64%
Then we wouldn't have had to write a, a a

00:15:38.463 --> 00:15:40.920 position:81%
compiler because there are compilers out there that.

00:15:41.240 --> 00:15:41.680
So why?

00:15:41.680 --> 00:15:42.200
Why?

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:43.440
Why didn't we do that?

00:15:44.800 --> 00:15:48.540 position:73%
Oh, there's a couple of reasons, and one of the

00:15:48.620 --> 00:15:52.519 position:77%
reasons is that we we we need a sandbox language.

00:15:52.880 --> 00:15:56.364 position:83%
Since we are running third party code on our servers,

00:15:56.430 --> 00:15:59.520 position:73%
we need something that we could control to the.

00:15:59.520 --> 00:16:00.880
Security reasons, for example.

00:16:00.880 --> 00:16:01.680
Yes, so.

00:16:01.680 --> 00:16:03.925 position:58%
So we it's not an option for us to go

00:16:03.986 --> 00:16:05.200
directly to dot net.

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:09.644 position:75%
We need to somehow use the language as a barrier

00:16:09.727 --> 00:16:13.731 position:75%
or having a trusted runtime for it that that was

00:16:13.815 --> 00:16:15.399
one of the reasons.

00:16:15.440 --> 00:16:18.801 position:67%
The other reason is that we have, I mean we

00:16:18.879 --> 00:16:23.178 position:86%
could go through the process of converting our own pace

00:16:23.256 --> 00:16:25.680
to any language potentially it.

00:16:25.760 --> 00:16:26.680
That's still a lot of work.

00:16:26.920 --> 00:16:29.908 position:66%
Still a lot of work and the result may not

00:16:29.980 --> 00:16:33.253 position:72%
be as easy to work with and actually would end

00:16:33.324 --> 00:16:37.523 position:92%
up not having the same connection between data and metadata

00:16:37.594 --> 00:16:38.519
that we have.

00:16:39.360 --> 00:16:42.062 position:69%
I mean we have the which is rather unique in

00:16:42.124 --> 00:16:42.799
my opinion.

00:16:42.800 --> 00:16:43.280
You can.

00:16:43.960 --> 00:16:45.877 position:66%
You can stand on a table field and you can

00:16:45.923 --> 00:16:48.480 position:88%
get cross references for all the places where it's used.

00:16:48.760 --> 00:16:51.049 position:78%
This is a language that's been written for for ERP

00:16:51.095 --> 00:16:52.239
and for Business Central.

00:16:52.240 --> 00:16:52.920
Exactly.

00:16:53.880 --> 00:16:56.359 position:75%
But the most important part of this was that our

00:16:56.410 --> 00:16:59.199 position:84%
entire partner channel have invested in this language.

00:16:59.760 --> 00:17:04.352 position:86%
So we actually have gone through great work to preserve

00:17:04.435 --> 00:17:08.360 position:73%
as much of their IP as possible in the process.

00:17:08.840 --> 00:17:11.468 position:69%
We know that from time to time, yes, we have

00:17:11.528 --> 00:17:13.560
asked our channel to make changes.

00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:16.574 position:77%
We don't like it and we know that they definitely

00:17:16.618 --> 00:17:17.239
don't like it.

00:17:17.240 --> 00:17:18.640
We do have breaking changes, but.

00:17:19.080 --> 00:17:19.760
We do have.

00:17:19.920 --> 00:17:21.800
We do what we can to preserve.

00:17:22.160 --> 00:17:24.837 position:66%
I mean if you look at the whole history of

00:17:24.901 --> 00:17:28.216 position:81%
of of NAV and Business Central, we've went through a

00:17:28.280 --> 00:17:31.851 position:88%
lot of efforts to preserve this code base throughout all

00:17:31.914 --> 00:17:32.679
these years.

00:17:32.680 --> 00:17:35.541 position:78%
Maybe that could be the topic of another under the

00:17:35.598 --> 00:17:36.799
hood talks, actually.

00:17:36.960 --> 00:17:40.277 position:78%
Yeah, it is actually something we have spent a lot

00:17:40.343 --> 00:17:42.399
of time on over the years, yes.

00:17:43.400 --> 00:17:47.094 position:63%
So you you said that that we're not a AL

00:17:47.186 --> 00:17:49.679
is not a true NET language.

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:54.160 position:86%
However, the L compiler is not is not exactly compiler.

00:17:54.400 --> 00:17:57.845 position:78%
We're not compiling in the pure sense of the word,

00:17:57.914 --> 00:18:02.255 position:98%
meaning we're not producing executable bytes and bits and bytes

00:18:02.324 --> 00:18:06.114 position:86%
and artifacts that can that can be executed directly on

00:18:06.183 --> 00:18:08.320
the on on the operating system.

00:18:08.560 --> 00:18:10.240
We it's more of a transpire, right?

00:18:10.240 --> 00:18:12.320
We are converting AL to C#.

00:18:12.840 --> 00:18:13.680
Is that a good idea?

00:18:14.800 --> 00:18:17.540 position:58%
Yes, I think for us it is we, we this

00:18:17.614 --> 00:18:20.280 position:56%
is what we did originally in C side.

00:18:20.320 --> 00:18:21.520
So C side did the same thing.

00:18:21.520 --> 00:18:23.120
Yes, C side did the same thing.

00:18:23.119 --> 00:18:27.956 position:77%
And when we started out, we we couldn't also take

00:18:28.054 --> 00:18:32.200 position:66%
upon the task too compiled directly to IL.

00:18:32.200 --> 00:18:32.600
It's hard.

00:18:32.640 --> 00:18:34.889 position:70%
It would be harder for us to verify, it would

00:18:34.939 --> 00:18:36.239
be harder for us to debug.

00:18:36.600 --> 00:18:37.840
So we chose that.

00:18:38.880 --> 00:18:41.555 position:67%
If we just did what C sent did, the runtime

00:18:41.617 --> 00:18:45.040 position:86%
would actually work, even though we had a new compiler.

00:18:45.120 --> 00:18:45.920
So what what happened?

00:18:45.920 --> 00:18:49.278 position:77%
When I have a snippet of code, the compiler takes

00:18:49.347 --> 00:18:52.980 position:83%
it and the L compiler takes it and actually generates

00:18:53.049 --> 00:18:56.545 position:80%
some C# code that basically does the same thing but

00:18:56.613 --> 00:18:59.561 position:67%
in C#, and then we compile the C code after

00:18:59.629 --> 00:19:03.400 position:86%
that to an actual execute using the regular C compiler,

00:19:03.468 --> 00:19:03.879
right?

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:06.080 position:64%
Yes, and that's exactly what we're doing.

00:19:06.600 --> 00:19:08.560 position:66%
And then we have some additional metadata.

00:19:08.560 --> 00:19:13.922 position:75%
We actually put in most cases XML and also stall

00:19:14.034 --> 00:19:18.280 position:59%
Mac in the runtime or in the database.

00:19:18.280 --> 00:19:20.440 position:58%
So this is what we use for execution.

00:19:21.359 --> 00:19:24.962 position:59%
So back to VS Code and back to the the

00:19:25.057 --> 00:19:29.039 position:66%
paradigm shift from from Seaside to to AL.

00:19:29.360 --> 00:19:30.200
There was another.

00:19:30.400 --> 00:19:32.695 position:63%
So we we went from from Seaside to to BS

00:19:32.753 --> 00:19:33.040
Code.

00:19:33.040 --> 00:19:34.280
That was 1 paradigm shift.

00:19:34.280 --> 00:19:38.829 position:98%
But there was another pretty significant paradigm shift that we

00:19:38.901 --> 00:19:39.840
went through.

00:19:39.840 --> 00:19:45.215 position:81%
At that stage we went from a repository based source

00:19:45.318 --> 00:19:50.279 position:75%
source artifacts to a text file based artifacts.

00:19:50.280 --> 00:19:53.446 position:77%
So before in Seaside when you, the code you wrote

00:19:53.510 --> 00:19:56.159 position:64%
was actually stored in the database, yes?

00:19:56.840 --> 00:20:00.442 position:77%
And now with with Visual Studio Code and with the

00:20:00.516 --> 00:20:04.266 position:80%
L compiler, the code is actually saved in text file

00:20:04.339 --> 00:20:08.530 position:89%
like any like many other languages like C# and JavaScript

00:20:08.604 --> 00:20:09.560
and so forth.

00:20:09.760 --> 00:20:11.640 position:64%
Is that is that was that a good thing or?

00:20:12.120 --> 00:20:14.680 position:80%
Yeah, in my opinion it was definitely a good thing.

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:18.401 position:66%
I earlier on in my tenure in Navy I worked

00:20:18.490 --> 00:20:23.895 position:95%
on modernizing the build pipeline for for the application and

00:20:23.983 --> 00:20:28.148 position:73%
and me and a colleague was taskbar to work with

00:20:28.236 --> 00:20:28.680
that.

00:20:28.680 --> 00:20:32.869 position:72%
And at that point in time all the sources were

00:20:32.960 --> 00:20:37.787 position:83%
exported from Seaside into the TXT file and then they

00:20:37.878 --> 00:20:42.796 position:84%
had to use a small B application that using automation

00:20:42.888 --> 00:20:48.352 position:94%
on top of Seaside to create database, import object, compile

00:20:48.443 --> 00:20:52.360 position:67%
all objects which was horrible to automate.

00:20:53.000 --> 00:20:55.947 position:70%
So the first the thing we actually did was to

00:20:56.013 --> 00:20:59.484 position:83%
just turn Seaside into give Seaside a command line so

00:20:59.550 --> 00:21:02.760 position:77%
you could actually compile from the command line.

00:21:03.680 --> 00:21:05.826 position:72%
It was not pretty, but it was better than what

00:21:05.873 --> 00:21:06.199
it was.

00:21:06.200 --> 00:21:09.518 position:77%
And from that point on I just dreamt about fixing

00:21:09.585 --> 00:21:12.565 position:69%
this for real because it was it was not nice

00:21:12.633 --> 00:21:13.920
to to work with it.

00:21:14.040 --> 00:21:16.784 position:83%
Well, and there there's a lot of advantages of having

00:21:16.835 --> 00:21:19.424 position:78%
the source code in texts, because there's a lot of

00:21:19.476 --> 00:21:22.479 position:91%
tools out there which you know, like source source control

00:21:22.530 --> 00:21:24.860 position:70%
to like git or and and you know, searching if

00:21:24.912 --> 00:21:27.760 position:86%
you want to search through the through the code, right?

00:21:27.760 --> 00:21:29.440 position:75%
There's a lot of search tools and you know that.

00:21:29.640 --> 00:21:30.000
Yes.

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:31.520
And when you work, yeah.

00:21:31.520 --> 00:21:35.961 position:83%
And when you work with, with repositories like Git or

00:21:36.045 --> 00:21:40.320 position:80%
DevOps, we also now have syntax highlight in there.

00:21:40.359 --> 00:21:43.887 position:94%
So you can actually better, yeah exactly inspect your source

00:21:43.946 --> 00:21:44.239
code.

00:21:44.240 --> 00:21:45.840 position:61%
So there's a lot of benefits from that.

00:21:46.040 --> 00:21:49.612 position:73%
And also there wasn't, I mean there was sort of

00:21:49.688 --> 00:21:53.716 position:83%
a parallel effort to our cloud endeavor at that point

00:21:53.792 --> 00:21:54.400
in time.

00:21:54.840 --> 00:21:57.220 position:70%
But it also turns out that, I mean having the

00:21:57.273 --> 00:22:00.182 position:86%
requirement to talk directly to a database in the cloud

00:22:00.235 --> 00:22:01.240
is a very bad idea.

00:22:01.240 --> 00:22:04.760
And so it it matches that as well.

00:22:06.480 --> 00:22:09.567 position:89%
So another thing that happened when we moved from Seaside

00:22:09.621 --> 00:22:11.679 position:59%
to Visual Studio Code is that we lost.

