these designers disappeared.

00:22:18.480 --> 00:22:21.183 position:78%
We went to again a fully text based approach where

00:22:21.237 --> 00:22:24.913 position:97%
instead of describing things through these designers, you would have

00:22:24.967 --> 00:22:27.616 position:77%
to describe them in, in in the text file directly

00:22:27.670 --> 00:22:29.941 position:66%
in the in the file where you had your code

00:22:29.995 --> 00:22:30.320
right.

00:22:31.560 --> 00:22:34.313 position:58%
And we we we got a lot of feedback on

00:22:34.387 --> 00:22:34.759
this.

00:22:34.840 --> 00:22:36.760
People are still asking about it.

00:22:37.960 --> 00:22:41.775 position:83%
So, but yeah, then you had another another great idea

00:22:41.847 --> 00:22:42.639
about that.

00:22:42.960 --> 00:22:43.840
Yes, I have.

00:22:44.119 --> 00:22:47.148 position:70%
The last couple of years I have focused a lot

00:22:47.216 --> 00:22:50.110 position:67%
on trying to figure out how we can make our

00:22:50.177 --> 00:22:54.619 position:97%
biggest API which is our basic, more visible, better discoverable,

00:22:54.686 --> 00:22:55.359
navigable.

00:22:56.280 --> 00:22:59.685 position:69%
And one of the ideas I wanted to explore was

00:22:59.763 --> 00:23:03.401 position:73%
if if we somehow could use the Web view feature

00:23:03.478 --> 00:23:08.200 position:95%
in Visual Studio Code in combination with our language server

00:23:08.278 --> 00:23:11.374 position:63%
to to actually put some UI on top of our

00:23:11.451 --> 00:23:13.000
application objects.

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:15.119 position:69%
So just to explain a little bit, the the web

00:23:15.167 --> 00:23:17.864 position:88%
view feature in Visual Studio Code, it's actually a page

00:23:17.912 --> 00:23:20.513 position:84%
that you can have like normally in Visual Studio Code,

00:23:20.561 --> 00:23:22.873 position:75%
when you open a file, it's typically a text file

00:23:22.921 --> 00:23:24.800 position:61%
that will show the content of the file.

00:23:24.800 --> 00:23:28.434 position:83%
But the Visual Studio Code has this feature where you

00:23:28.502 --> 00:23:31.657 position:72%
can actually have a page which is in essence a

00:23:31.725 --> 00:23:34.400 position:61%
browser and you can display HTML in it.

00:23:34.400 --> 00:23:38.961 position:73%
So you can display pretty advanced UI in it and

00:23:39.059 --> 00:23:44.882 position:94%
this page can communicate with other regular text pages like

00:23:44.979 --> 00:23:48.279
for example you can have you know.

00:23:48.400 --> 00:23:50.699 position:72%
A good example is that I think many people are

00:23:50.749 --> 00:23:51.599
familiar with is.

00:23:51.600 --> 00:23:54.059 position:61%
If you open a read me file, you can see

00:23:54.122 --> 00:23:57.023 position:72%
the markup language in a text file and you can

00:23:57.086 --> 00:24:00.239 position:78%
see the preview of it in the actual formatted way.

00:24:00.960 --> 00:24:03.720 position:88%
They actually have Visual Studio Code have two concepts.

00:24:03.720 --> 00:24:07.246 position:80%
They have one which is a web view disconnected from

00:24:07.315 --> 00:24:07.799
source.

00:24:07.880 --> 00:24:12.674 position:80%
Then they have the custom design, I think web view,

00:24:12.768 --> 00:24:17.844 position:84%
and the what we shipped first was actually AL Explorer

00:24:17.938 --> 00:24:19.160
and ALE Home.

00:24:19.200 --> 00:24:22.240 position:81%
They are built on just being a web view disconnected

00:24:22.298 --> 00:24:23.000
from source.

00:24:23.720 --> 00:24:27.076 position:80%
But the AL Explorer is using actually the same grid

00:24:27.141 --> 00:24:30.300 position:75%
